(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["cross-text-connections_3"] = [{"id":"97e5bf55","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 In 1916, H. Dugdale Sykes disputed claims that The Two Noble Kinsmen was coauthored by William Shakespeare and John Fletcher. Sykes felt Fletcher’s contributions to the play were obvious — Fletcher had a distinct style in his other plays, so much so that lines with that style were considered sufficient evidence of Fletcher’s authorship. But for the lines not deemed to be by Fletcher, Sykes felt that their depiction of women indicated that their author was not Shakespeare but Philip Massinger. Text 2 Scholars have accepted The Two Noble Kinsmen as coauthored by Shakespeare since the 1970s: it appears in all major one-volume editions of Shakespeare’s complete works. Though scholars disagree about who wrote what exactly, it is generally held that on the basis of style, Shakespeare wrote all of the first act and most of the last, while John Fletcher authored most of the three middle acts.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 1916, H. Dugdale Sykes disputed claims that The Two Noble Kinsmen was coauthored by William Shakespeare and John Fletcher. Sykes felt","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Fletcher’s contributions to the play were obvious—Fletcher had a distinct style in his other plays, so much so that lines with that style were","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"considered sufficient evidence of Fletcher’s authorship. But for the lines not deemed to be by Fletcher, Sykes felt that their depiction of women","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"indicated that their author was not Shakespeare but Philip Massinger.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Scholars have accepted The Two Noble Kinsmen as coauthored by Shakespeare since the 1970s: it appears in all major one-volume editions of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Shakespeare’s complete works. Though scholars disagree about who wrote what exactly, it is generally held that on the basis of style,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Shakespeare wrote all of the first act and most of the last, while John Fletcher authored most of the three middle acts.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, both Sykes in Text 1 and the scholars in Text 2 would most likely agree with which statement?","choices":["John Fletcher’s writing has a unique, readily identifiable style.","The women characters in John Fletcher’s plays are similar to the women characters in Philip Massinger’s plays.’","The Two Noble Kinsmen belongs in one-volume compilations of Shakespeare’s complete plays.","Philip Massinger’s style in the first and last acts of The Two Noble Kinsmen is an homage to Shakespeare’s style."]},{"id":"d0198544","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 In 2007, a team led by Alice Storey analyzed a chicken bone found in El Arenal, Chile, dating it to 1321 –1407 CE — over a century before Europeans invaded the region, bringing their own chickens. Storey also found that the El Arenal chicken shared a unique genetic mutation with the ancient chicken breeds of the Polynesian Islands in the Pacific. Thus, Polynesian peoples, not later Europeans, probably first introduced chickens to South America. Text 2 An Australian research team weakened the case for a Polynesian origin for the El Arenal chicken by confirming that the mutation identified by Storey has occurred in breeds from around the world. More recently, though, a team led by Agusto Luzuriaga-Neira found that South American chicken breeds and Polynesian breeds share other genetic markers that European breeds lack. Thus, the preponderance of evidence now favors a Polynesian origin.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 2007, a team led by Alice Storey analyzed a chicken bone found in El Arenal, Chile, dating it to 1321–1407 CE—over a century before","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Europeans invaded the region, bringing their own chickens. Storey also found that the El Arenal chicken shared a unique genetic mutation with","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the ancient chicken breeds of the Polynesian Islands in the Pacific. Thus, Polynesian peoples, not later Europeans, probably first introduced","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"chickens to South America.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"An Australian research team weakened the case for a Polynesian origin for the El Arenal chicken by confirming that the mutation identified by","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Storey has occurred in breeds from around the world. More recently, though, a team led by Agusto Luzuriaga-Neira found that South American","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"chicken breeds and Polynesian breeds share other genetic markers that European breeds lack. Thus, the preponderance of evidence now favors","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"a Polynesian origin.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?","choices":["By broadly agreeing with the claim but objecting that the timeline it presupposes conflicts with the findings of the genetic analysis conducted by Storey’s team","By faulting the claim for implying that domestic animals couldn’t have been transferred from South America to the Polynesian Islands as well","By critiquing the claim for being based on an assumption that before the European invasion of South America, the chickens of Europe were genetically uniform","By noting that while the claim is persuasive, the findings of Luzuriaga-Neira’s team provide stronger evidence for it than the findings of the genetic analysis conducted by Storey do"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Polynesian peoples, not later Europeans, probably first introduced","chickens to South America."]},{"id":"ed52a093","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Flamingos are known for their vibrant pink coloring, but they’re actually born with gray feathers. Their pink color comes from eating brine shrimp, but brine shrimp aren’t naturally pink either. Animals can’t produce carotenoids, the pigments that provide the pink hue. The algae that brine shrimp feed on, however, can produce these pigments. Thus, the pinker the flamingo, the more shrimp it has eaten. Text 2 Ecologist Juan Amat has found that flamingos apply a kind of makeup to make themselves appear pinker. A gland near their tail contains pigments that come from the food they eat. When the flamingos groom themselves using the pigments, their feathers become pinker. Flamingos may do this to improve their success during mating season, when they would benefit from looking pinker.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Flamingos are known for their vibrant pink coloring, but they’re actually born with gray feathers. Their pink color comes from eating brine shrimp,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"but brine shrimp aren’t naturally pink either. Animals can’t produce carotenoids, the pigments that provide the pink hue. The algae that brine","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"shrimp feed on, however, can produce these pigments. Thus, the pinker the flamingo, the more shrimp it has eaten.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Ecologist Juan Amat has found that flamingos apply a kind of makeup to make themselves appear pinker. A gland near their tail contains","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pigments that come from the food they eat. When the flamingos groom themselves using the pigments, their feathers become pinker. Flamingos","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"may do this to improve their success during mating season, when they would benefit from looking pinker.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the ecologist in Text 2 most likely respond to the author’s conclusion in Text 1?","choices":["By emphasizing that flamingos’ tail feathers are pinker than their other feathers are","By claiming that the coloring of flamingos’ feathers doesn’t change significantly enough for most observers to notice","By pointing out that the amount of shrimp eaten isn’t the only thing that influences flamingos’ coloring","By arguing that flamingos’ diet doesn’t include much shrimp except during mating season"]},{"id":"835d1ae6","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Historians studying pre-Inca Peru have looked to ceramic vessels to understand daily life among the Moche people. These mold-made sculptures present plants, animals, and human faces in precise ways — vessels representing human faces are so detailed that scholars have interpreted facial markings to represent scars and other skin irregularities. Some historians have even used these objects to identify potential skin diseases that may have afflicted people at the time. Text 2 Art historian and archaeologist Lisa Trever has argued that the interpretation of Moche “portrait” vessels as hyper-realistic portrayals of identifiable people may inadvertently disregard the creativity of the objects creators. Moche ceramic vessels, Trever argues, are artworks in which sculptors could free their imagination, using realistic objects and people around them as inspiration to explore more abstract concepts. Based on the texts,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Historians studying pre-Inca Peru have looked to ceramic vessels to understand daily life among the Moche people. These mold-made sculptures","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"present plants, animals, and human faces in precise ways—vessels representing human faces are so detailed that scholars have interpreted","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"facial markings to represent scars and other skin irregularities. Some historians have even used these objects to identify potential skin diseases","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that may have afflicted people at the time.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Art historian and archaeologist Lisa Trever has argued that the interpretation of Moche “portrait” vessels as hyper-realistic portrayals of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"identifiable people may inadvertently disregard the creativity of the objects’ creators. Moche ceramic vessels, Trever argues, are artworks in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"which sculptors could free their imagination, using realistic objects and people around them as inspiration to explore more abstract concepts.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, what would Lisa Trever (Text 2) most likely say about the interpretation presented in the underlined portion of Text 1?","choices":["Depictions of human faces are significantly more realistic than depictions of plants and other animals are.","It is likely that some depictions of human faces with extensive markings are intended to portray the same historical individual.","Some vessels may have been damaged during their excavation and thus provide little insight into Moche culture.","Markings on depictions of human faces are not necessarily intended to portray particular details about the physical appearance of individuals."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["scholars have interpreted","facial markings to represent scars and other skin irregularities."]},{"id":"9a94eb77","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 One challenge faced by researchers studying global urbanization is that countries may define urban settlements differently. Many countries define urban settlements","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"One challenge faced by researchers studying global urbanization is that countries may define urban settlements differently. Many countries","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"define urban settlements based on the number of people living in them. However, countries do not all use the same number; for example, Iceland","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"uses a minimum population of 200, while the Netherlands uses 20,000. Other countries use a combination of population and other factors. This","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"variation makes it difficult for researchers to compare aspects of urbanization in different countries.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Recently, a group of six international organizations developed global definitions of common types of settlements. The group developed a new","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"measure called the “degree of urbanization.” This new measure establishes global criteria used to define three types of settlements (cities,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"towns, and rural areas) and allows researchers to better understand global urbanization rates.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the problem presented in Text 1?","choices":["By recommending that a specific institution should further investigate the problem","By suggesting that researchers focus on topics besides urbanization","By supplying additional ways in which urbanization research is difficult","By noting that a possible solution to the problem is available"]},{"id":"de2c2f57","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 The fossil record suggests that mammoths went extinct around 11 thousand years (kyr) ago. In a 2021 study of environmental DNA (eDNA) — genetic material shed into the environment by organisms — in the Arctic, Yucheng Wang and colleagues found mammoth eDNA in sedimentary layers formed millennia later, around 4 kyr ago. To account for this discrepancy, Joshua H. Miller and Carl Simpson proposed that arctic temperatures could preserve a mammoth carcass on the surface, allowing it to leach DNA into the environment, for several thousand years. Text 2 Wang and colleagues concede that eDNA contains DNA from both living organisms and carcasses, but for DNA to leach from remains over several millennia requires that the remains be perpetually on the surface. Scavengers and weathering in the Arctic, however, are likely to break down surface remains well before a thousand years have passed.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The fossil record suggests that mammoths went extinct around 11 thousand years (kyr) ago. In a 2021 study of environmental DNA (eDNA)—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"genetic material shed into the environment by organisms—in the Arctic, Yucheng Wang and colleagues found mammoth eDNA in sedimentary","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"layers formed millennia later, around 4 kyr ago. To account for this discrepancy, Joshua H. Miller and Carl Simpson proposed that arctic","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"temperatures could preserve a mammoth carcass on the surface, allowing it to leach DNA into the environment, for several thousand years.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Wang and colleagues concede that eDNA contains DNA from both living organisms and carcasses, but for DNA to leach from remains over","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"several millennia requires that the remains be perpetually on the surface. Scavengers and weathering in the Arctic, however, are likely to break","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"down surface remains well before a thousand years have passed.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes how Text 1 and Text 2 relate to each other?","choices":["Text 1 discusses two approaches to studying mammoth extinction without advocating for either, whereas Text 2 advocates for one approach over the other.","Text 1 presents findings by Wang and colleagues and gives another research team’s attempt to explain those findings, whereas Text 2 provides additional detail that calls that explanation into question.","Text 1 describes Wang and colleagues study and a critique of their methodology, whereas Text 2 offers additional details showing that methodology to be sound.","Text 1 argues that new research has undermined the standard view of when mammoths went extinct, whereas Text 2 suggests a way to reconcile the standard view with that new research."]},{"id":"5a4b147c","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 On April 26th, 1777, Sybil Ludington rode 40 miles by horse through Putnam County, New York, to gather up local militia. British forces were burning nearby Danbury, Connecticut, and Ludington wanted to rally rebel troops to meet them. Although she was only 16 years old at the time, her brave feat made Ludington one of the heroes of the American Revolution. Since then, Ludington has been widely celebrated, inspiring postage stamps, statues, and even children’s TV series. Text 2 Historian Paula D. Hunt researched the life and legacy of Sybil Ludington but found no evidence for her famous ride. Although many articles and books have been written about Ludington, Hunt believes writers may have been inventing details about Ludington as they retold her story. Ludington is revered by Americans today, but there simply isn’t a strong historical record of her heroic ride.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"On April 26th, 1777, Sybil Ludington rode 40 miles by horse through Putnam County, New York, to gather up local militia. British forces were","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"burning nearby Danbury, Connecticut, and Ludington wanted to rally rebel troops to meet them. Although she was only 16 years old at the time,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"her brave feat made Ludington one of the heroes of the American Revolution. Since then, Ludington has been widely celebrated, inspiring","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"postage stamps, statues, and even children’s TV series.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Historian Paula D. Hunt researched the life and legacy of Sybil Ludington but found no evidence for her famous ride. Although many articles and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"books have been written about Ludington, Hunt believes writers may have been inventing details about Ludington as they retold her story.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Ludington is revered by Americans today, but there simply isn’t a strong historical record of her heroic ride.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?","choices":["Sybil Ludington was crucial to the outcome of the Revolutionary War.","Historians have confirmed which route Sybil Ludington took.","Sybil Ludington was likely not a real person.","Many people have come to admire the story of Sybil Ludington’s ride."]},{"id":"c106b9f7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 American sculptor Edmonia Lewis is best known for her sculptures that represent figures from history and mythology, such as The Death of Cleopatra and Hagar. Although Lewis sculpted other subjects, her career as a sculptor is best represented by the works in which she depicted these historical and mythical themes. Text 2 Art historians have typically ignored the many portrait busts Edmonia Lewis created. Lewis likely carved these busts (sculptures of a person’s head) frequently throughout her long career. She is known for her sculptures that represent historical figures, but Lewis likely supported herself financially by carving portrait busts for acquaintances who paid her to represent their features. Thus, Lewis’s portrait busts are a central aspect of her career as a sculptor.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"American sculptor Edmonia Lewis is best known for her sculptures that represent figures from history and mythology, such as The Death of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Cleopatra and Hagar. Although Lewis sculpted other subjects, her career as a sculptor is best represented by the works in which she depicted","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"these historical and mythical themes.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Art historians have typically ignored the many portrait busts Edmonia Lewis created. Lewis likely carved these busts (sculptures of a person’s","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"head) frequently throughout her long career. She is known for her sculptures that represent historical figures, but Lewis likely supported herself","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"financially by carving portrait busts for acquaintances who paid her to represent their features. Thus, Lewis’s portrait busts are a central aspect","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of her career as a sculptor.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree with which statement?","choices":["Lewis’s portrait busts have overshadowed her other work.","The Death of Cleopatra is Lewis’s most famous piece.","Sculpting representations of historical figures was a short-lived trend.","Lewis’s works are varied in the subjects they depict."]},{"id":"f52cc78c","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Polar bears sustain themselves primarily by hunting seals on the Arctic sea ice, but rising ocean temperatures are causing the ice to diminish, raising concerns about polar bear population declines as these large predators’ seal-hunting habitats continue to shrink. A 2020 study examining polar bear populations across the Arctic concluded that populations affected by sea-ice loss are at great risk of extinction by the end of the twenty-first century. Text 2 Monitoring carried out by researchers from the Norwegian Polar Institute shows that the polar bear population on the Arctic archipelago of Svalbard remains stable and well nourished despite rapidly declining sea ice in recent years. The researchers attribute this population’s resilience in part to a shift in feeding strategies: in addition to hunting seals, the Svalbard polar bears have begun relying on a diet of reindeer meat and birds’ eggs.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Polar bears sustain themselves primarily by hunting seals on the Arctic sea ice, but rising ocean temperatures are causing the ice to diminish,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"raising concerns about polar bear population declines as these large predators’ seal-hunting habitats continue to shrink. A 2020 study examining","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"polar bear populations across the Arctic concluded that populations affected by sea-ice loss are at great risk of extinction by the end of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"twenty-first century.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Monitoring carried out by researchers from the Norwegian Polar Institute shows that the polar bear population on the Arctic archipelago of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Svalbard remains stable and well nourished despite rapidly declining sea ice in recent years. The researchers attribute this population’s resilience","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in part to a shift in feeding strategies: in addition to hunting seals, the Svalbard polar bears have begun relying on a diet of reindeer meat and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"birds’ eggs.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the researchers in Text 2 most likely respond to the conclusion presented in the underlined portion of Text 1?","choices":["By noting that it neglects the possibility of some polar bear populations adapting to changes in their environment","By suggesting that it is likely incorrect about the rates at which warming ocean temperatures have caused sea ice to melt in the Arctic","By asserting that it overlooks polar bear populations that have not yet been affected by loss of seal-hunting habitats","By arguing that it fails to account for polar bears’ reliance on a single seal-hunting strategy"],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["concluded that populations affected by sea-ice loss are at great risk of extinction by the end of the","twenty-first century."]},{"id":"eae66bf9","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 In 2021, a team led by Amir Siraj hypothesized that the Chicxulub impactor — the object that struck the Yucat án Peninsula sixty-six million years ago, precipitating the mass extinction of the dinosaurs — was likely a member of the class of long-period comets. As evidence, Siraj cited the carbonaceous chondritic composition of samples from the Chicxulub impact crater as well as of samples obtained from long-period comet Wild 2 in 2006. Text 2 Although long-period comets contain carbonaceous chondrites, asteroids are similarly rich in these materials. Furthermore, some asteroids are rich in iridium, as Natalia Artemieva points out, whereas long-period comets are not. Given the prevalence of iridium at the crater and, more broadly, in geological layers deposited worldwide following the impact, Artemieva argues that an asteroid is a more plausible candidate for the Chicxulub impactor.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"In 2021, a team led by Amir Siraj hypothesized that the Chicxulub impactor—the object that struck the Yucatán Peninsula sixty-six million years","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"ago, precipitating the mass extinction of the dinosaurs—was likely a member of the class of long-period comets. As evidence, Siraj cited the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"carbonaceous chondritic composition of samples from the Chicxulub impact crater as well as of samples obtained from long-period comet Wild","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"2 in 2006.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Although long-period comets contain carbonaceous chondrites, asteroids are similarly rich in these materials. Furthermore, some asteroids are","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"rich in iridium, as Natalia Artemieva points out, whereas long-period comets are not. Given the prevalence of iridium at the crater and, more","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"broadly, in geological layers deposited worldwide following the impact, Artemieva argues that an asteroid is a more plausible candidate for the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Chicxulub impactor.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would Artemieva likely respond to Siraj’s hypothesis, as presented in Text 1?","choices":["By insisting that it overestimates how representative Wild 2 is of long-period comets as a class","By arguing that it does not account for the amount of iridium found in geological layers dating to the Chicxulub impact","By praising it for connecting the composition of Chicxulub crater samples to the composition of certain asteroids","By concurring that carbonaceous chondrites are prevalent in soil samples from sites distant from the Chicxulub crater"]},{"id":"02fd3da7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Public policy researcher Anthony Fowler studied the history of elections in Australia, a country that requires citizens to vote. Fowler argues that requiring citizens to vote leads to a significant increase in voters who would otherwise not have the time or motivation to vote. Thus, election results in countries that require citizens to vote better reflect the preferences of the country as a whole. Text 2 Governments in democratic countries function better when more people vote. However, forcing people to vote may have negative consequences. Shane P. Singh and Jason Roy studied what happens when a country requires its citizens to vote. They found that when people feel forced to vote, they tend to spend less time looking for information about their choices when voting. As a result, votes from these voters may not reflect their actual preferences.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Public policy researcher Anthony Fowler studied the history of elections in Australia, a country that requires citizens to vote. Fowler argues that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"requiring citizens to vote leads to a significant increase in voters who would otherwise not have the time or motivation to vote. Thus, election","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"results in countries that require citizens to vote better reflect the preferences of the country as a whole.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Governments in democratic countries function better when more people vote. However, forcing people to vote may have negative consequences.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Shane P. Singh and Jason Roy studied what happens when a country requires its citizens to vote. They found that when people feel forced to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"vote, they tend to spend less time looking for information about their choices when voting. As a result, votes from these voters may not reflect","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"their actual preferences.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would Singh and Roy (Text 2) most likely respond to the research discussed in Text 1?","choices":["Only countries of a certain population size should implement mandatory voting.","People who are forced to vote are likely to become politically engaged in other ways, such as volunteering or running for office.","Requiring people to vote does not necessarily lead to election outcomes that better represent the preferences of the country as a whole.","Countries that require voting must also make the process of voting easier for their citizens."]},{"id":"5e4e082c","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Chemically analyzing bulk rock-core samples from Australia’s Mount McRae Shale, Ariel Anbar et al. found a transient but significant increase in molybdenum (Mo) at a point corresponding to roughly 2.5 billion years ago (Ga). On Earth, Mo is released mainly through oxidative weathering of minerals; Anbar et al. therefore concluded that atmospheric oxygen briefly increased 2.5 Ga, then returned to its earlier negligible level. Text 2 Sarah Slotznick et al. reexamined the Mount McRae Shale core. Since chemically analyzing bulk samples can occlude contextual details, Slotznick et al. also employed high-resolution microscopy, which revealed volcanic debris — a known Mo host —around 2.5 Ga and microfractures in the surrounding matrix. The researchers assert that fluid could have reached the debris through the microfractures and initiated oxidative weathering long after debris deposition.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Chemically analyzing bulk rock-core samples from Australia’s Mount McRae Shale, Ariel Anbar et al. found a transient but significant increase in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"molybdenum (Mo) at a point corresponding to roughly 2.5 billion years ago (Ga). On Earth, Mo is released mainly through oxidative weathering of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"minerals; Anbar et al. therefore concluded that atmospheric oxygen briefly increased 2.5 Ga, then returned to its earlier negligible level.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Sarah Slotznick et al. reexamined the Mount McRae Shale core. Since chemically analyzing bulk samples can occlude contextual details,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Slotznick et al. also employed high-resolution microscopy, which revealed volcanic debris—a known Mo host—around 2.5 Ga and microfractures","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in the surrounding matrix. The researchers assert that fluid could have reached the debris through the microfractures and initiated oxidative","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"weathering long after debris deposition.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, Anbar et al. (Text 1) and Slotznick et al. (Text 2) would most likely disagree about the answer to which question about the portion of the Mount McRae Shale rock-core corresponding to 2.5 Ga?","choices":["Does the increase in Mo in that portion suggest that atmospheric oxygen levels were not negligible before 2.5 Ga?","Is the increase in Mo in that portion indicative of an increase in atmospheric oxygen dating to the same time?","Is the increase in Mo in that portion attributable to oxidative weathering of the material found in that portion?","Did chemically analyzing bulk samples lead to a false impression that there is an increase in Mo in that portion?"]},{"id":"c68ceeff","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Today the starchy root cassava is found in many dishes across West Africa, but its rise to popularity was slow. Portuguese traders brought cassava from Brazil to the West African coast in the 1500s. But at this time, people living in the capitals further inland had little contact with coastal communities. Thus, cassava remained relatively unknown to most of the region’s inhabitants until the 1800s. Text 2 Cassava’s slow adoption into the diet of West Africans is mainly due to the nature of the crop itself. If not cooked properly, cassava can be toxic. Knowledge of how to properly prepare cassava needed to spread before the food could grow in popularity. The arrival of formerly enslaved people from Brazil in the 1800s, who brought their knowledge of cassava and its preparation with them, thus directly fueled the spread of this crop.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Today the starchy root cassava is found in many dishes across West Africa, but its rise to popularity was slow. Portuguese traders brought","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cassava from Brazil to the West African coast in the 1500s. But at this time, people living in the capitals further inland had little contact with","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"coastal communities. Thus, cassava remained relatively unknown to most of the region’s inhabitants until the 1800s.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Cassava’s slow adoption into the diet of West Africans is mainly due to the nature of the crop itself. If not cooked properly, cassava can be toxic.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Knowledge of how to properly prepare cassava needed to spread before the food could grow in popularity. The arrival of formerly enslaved","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"people from Brazil in the 1800s, who brought their knowledge of cassava and its preparation with them, thus directly fueled the spread of this","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"crop.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 would most likely agree with which statement?","choices":["Cassava did not become a significant crop in West Africa until long after it was first introduced.","Several of the most commonly grown crops in West Africa are originally from Brazil.","The climate of the West African coast in the 1500s prevented cassava’s spread in the region.","The most commonly used methods to cook cassava today date to the 1500s."]}];
